<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mask of Agamemnon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Death Mask of Agamemnon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mask of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> is a gold funerary mask found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> in 1876 in Grave V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grave-circle-a">Grave Circle A</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Hammered from a single thick sheet of gold, it shows a face with a pointed beard and moustache. Schliemann named it after the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, but it dates to around the 16th century BCE, centuries before the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. Its authenticity has been questioned by William Calder III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Traill, but modern research suggests it is genuine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mask-of-agamemnon.jpg" alt="Mask of Agamemnon" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
